  "course_key": "g3-sec1-math",
  "unit_key": "g3-sec1-g5",
  "position": 3,
  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Volume and surface area of prism and cylinder",
  "summary": "Use cross-sections and nets to calculate prism and cylinder measures.",
  "outcomes": [
    "5.3"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "g5-lesson-02"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Calculate a prism volume from cross-sectional area and length.",
    "Calculate cylinder volume using πr²h.",
    "Build total surface area from end faces and lateral faces."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "g5-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-53-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Core method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use cross-sections and nets to calculate prism and cylinder measures. Keep every measurement in compatible units before substituting."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "V_{prism}=A_{cross-section}\\ell,\\qquad V_{cylinder}=\\pi r^2h"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-03-visual",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read the diagram",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The diagram shows a triangular prism. Its cross-section, the shaded right-angled triangle at the front, is the same all the way along its 9 cm length, so volume = cross-sectional area × length. A cylinder is a prism whose cross-section is a circle, so its volume is πr²h. Surface area unfolds every exposed face: for a closed solid, two equal ends plus the faces around the side."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g5-lesson-03-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-03-reasoning",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Reasoning method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For a right prism, the lateral area is perimeter of cross-section×length. For a cylinder, curved area=2πrh and total surface area=2πr²+2πrh. Check whether a container is open or closed before including circular ends."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-53-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: volume of a triangular prism",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The triangular prism in the diagram has a right-angled triangular cross-section with perpendicular sides 8 cm and 5 cm, and length 9 cm. Find its volume."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The cross-section is a right-angled triangle, so its area is ½ × base × height."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "A=\\frac12\\times8\\times5=20\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Multiply the cross-sectional area by the length of the prism."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "V=20\\times9=180\\text{ cm}^3"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The prism is half of an 8 cm by 5 cm by 9 cm cuboid, and 8 × 5 × 9 = 360, so half is 180 cm³."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-03-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: surface area of a closed cylinder",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A closed cylinder has radius 3 cm and height 8 cm. Find its total surface area in terms of π."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The two circular ends:"
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "2\\pi r^2=2\\pi(3)^2=18\\pi\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The curved surface unrolls into a rectangle 2πr long and h high:"
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "2\\pi rh=2\\pi(3)(8)=48\\pi\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\text{Total surface area}=18\\pi+48\\pi=66\\pi\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "A closed cylinder includes both ends, and every term is in square units."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-03-example-3",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: an open cylinder with π = 22/7",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The open cylindrical tin in the diagram has radius 7 cm and height 12 cm. Using "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "\\pi=\\frac{22}{7}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ", find (a) its volume and (b) the area of metal needed to make it."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g5-lesson-03-cylinder"
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "With r = 7, the value "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "latex": "\\pi=\\frac{22}{7}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " cancels neatly. The base area is"
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\pi r^2=\\frac{22}{7}\\times7\\times7=154\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "(a) Volume = base area × height."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "V=154\\times12=1848\\text{ cm}^3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "(b) The tin is open, so it has one circular end (the base) and a curved surface."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "2\\pi rh=2\\times\\frac{22}{7}\\times7\\times12=528\\text{ cm}^2"
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\text{Metal}=154+528=682\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Only one end is included because the top is open. Using the calculator's π instead of 22/7 changes only the later digits: 1847.3 cm³ instead of 1848 cm³."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-03-pitfalls",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors and checks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not confuse diameter with radius. Volume uses cubic units; surface area uses square units. For prisms, the cross-sectional area is multiplied by the prism length, which may not be the vertical dimension in the sketch."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-53-recall",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A prism has cross-sectional area 15 cm² and length 8 cm. Find its volume in cm³."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "120",
        "canonical_latex": "120",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Multiply cross-sectional area by length: 15 × 8 = 120."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-03-summary",
      "position": 9,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Method checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prism: volume = cross-sectional area × length. Cylinder: volume = πr²h and curved surface area = 2πrh."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For surface area, list every exposed face first: a closed cylinder has two circular ends, an open one has one. Halve a diameter before using r, and use "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "\\pi=\\frac{22}{7}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " or the calculator value as the question says."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
